BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:250th Trivia and Potluck at Ferrisburgh Town Hall
DTSTART:20261002T173000
DTEND:20261002T200000
DTSTAMP:20261001T172503Z
UID:af64dfba496082b465dd05b2c2610e@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:Vermont History Trivia is a great way to have fun with friends
  and family. Expertise isn't needed\; Questions are multiple choice to hel
 p both newbies and history buffs engage with our state's past! Bring a dis
 h\, a friend\, or just your own knowledge. No donation is necessary to par
 ticipate. All ages are welcome! The event is FREE and accessible to the pu
 blic. Prizes and questions are provided by the Vermont Historical Society.
  A special "Ferrisburgh History" round has been developed with the aid of 
 local historians. This event is hosted in partnership with the Vermont His
 torical Society\, Rokeby Museum\, Friends of the Union Meeting Hall\, and 
 the Rogers & Hazard Committee. Ferrisburgh Town Hall\n\nSources: Vermont P
 ublic
LOCATION:Vermont Public is independent\, community-supported media\, servi
 ng Vermont with trusted\, relevant and essential information. We share sto
 ries that bring people together\, from every corner of our region.\, © 20
 26 Vermont Public | 365 Troy Ave. Colchester\, VT 05446
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/250th-triv
 ia-and-potluck-at-ferrisburgh-town-hall-22-09-2026-09-19-35
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Duncan Johnson & Hannah Morris: Planes of Distinction
DTSTART:20261002T163000
DTEND:20261002T190000
DTSTAMP:20261001T172503Z
UID:2ad031697103b7992169c96f69730d@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:Planes of Distinction brings together the work of artists Dunc
 an Johnson and Hannah Morris in a dialogue that explores the subtle bounda
 ries between perception and place\, structure and atmosphere\, memory and 
 observation. Through distinct yet complementary approaches\, both artists 
 investigate how landscapes\, forms\, and spatial relationships shape our u
 nderstanding of the world around us. Johnson’s work examines the underly
 ing geometry and architecture of the visual environment\, distilling compl
 ex compositions into layers of line\, color\, and form using found objects
 \, collage\, and ephemera. Morris approaches the landscape through a more 
 intuitive lens\, capturing fleeting moods\, shifting light\, and the emoti
 onal resonance of people and place. Together\, the work of these two artis
 ts reveals how seemingly separate visual planes -- physical\, psychologica
 l\, and conceptual -- intersect and inform one another. The exhibition tit
 le\, Planes of Distinction\, refers not only to compositional surfaces and
  spatial divisions but also to the varied perspectives through which we ex
 perience the world. Each artist invites viewers to navigate these overlapp
 ing terrains\, discovering moments of tension\, harmony\, and transformati
 on. The result is a compelling conversation between two artistic perspecti
 ves\, each charting a unique course across visual planes. Free and open to
  the public. Food and drink served.\n\nSources: Vermont Public
LOCATION:Vermont Supreme Court Gallery\, 111 State Street
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/duncan-joh
 nson-hannah-morris-planes-of-distinction-17-09-2026-15-10-14
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Printmaker Jo Marsan of The Campy Bird Studio exhibiting at Artisa
 ns Hand during Montpelier Art Walk
DTSTART:20261002T160000
DTEND:20261002T190000
DTSTAMP:20261001T172503Z
UID:9c8d7763e61b3f66f881e960993220@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:During October's Montpelier Art Walk\, we’re featuring Montp
 elier printmaker Jo Marsan of The Campy Bird Studio. They’re the second 
 participant in our Emerging Artisan & Art Walk Program. Jo creates handmad
 e prints of native Vermont plants and animals with an emphasis on line-wor
 k\, contrast\, and vibrant color. They print using environmentally-friendl
 y water-based inks on 100% cotton paper. They received their BFA in printm
 aking from Ohio University before settling in Vermont. In addition to prin
 tmaking\, Jo has a background in graphic design and mural arts. If you're 
 ever in Morrisville\, be sure to check out their gorgeous mural at the Vil
 lage Center Apartments on Hutchins Street. Guidebooks are available in the
  gallery now\, so be sure to stop by and get yours! We look forward to see
 ing you!\n\nSources: Vermont Public
LOCATION:Artisans Hand Craft Gallery\, 89 Main Street at City Center
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/printmaker
 -jo-marsan-of-the-campy-bird-studio-exhibiting-at-artisans-hand-during-mon
 tpelier-art-walk-23-09-2026-16-43-30
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Rappahannock County
DTSTART:20261002T193000
DTEND:20261002T213000
DTSTAMP:20261001T172503Z
UID:35edaba94aad78280c487483ba4042@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:Our Fall Festival's cornerstone event\, Rappahannock County is
  an operatic tapestry of stories from the American Civil War\, with music 
 by renowned composer Ricky Ian Gordon and a libretto by Pulitzer Prize-win
 ner Mark Campbell. Premiered in 2011 and inspired by diaries\, letters and
  firsthand accounts\, this moving work gives voice to soldiers\, enslaved 
 people\, families\, and civilians whose lives were forever changed by the 
 nation's defining conflict. This moving work premiered in 2011\, inspired 
 by diaries\, letters\, and firsthand accounts\, gives voice to soldiers\, 
 enslaved people\, families\, and civilians whose lives were forever change
 d by the nation's defining conflict. Set in Virginia but resonating far be
 yond its borders\, this opera explores themes of freedom\, loss\, courage
 \, and reconciliation that remain deeply relevant today. With Gordon's lyr
 ical score and Campbell's poignant libretto\, Rappahannock County offers a
 n intimate reflection on the human cost of war and the enduring power of e
 mpathy and hope. Town Hall Theater\n\nSources: Vermont Public
LOCATION:Vermont Public is independent\, community-supported media\, servi
 ng Vermont with trusted\, relevant and essential information. We share sto
 ries that bring people together\, from every corner of our region.\, © 20
 26 Vermont Public | 365 Troy Ave. Colchester\, VT 05446
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/rappahanno
 ck-county-15-09-2026-13-55-55
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Work in Progress
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261002
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261003
DTSTAMP:20261001T172503Z
UID:6424f7edeec57a207cc809502ac7c9@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:Robert Reich follows up his acclaimed hit film The Last Class
 \, which documented his last inspiring final lecture at UC Berkeley\, with
  a timely and poignant snapshot of a sharply divided America. A team of fi
 lmmakers\, inspired by former Secretary of Labor Robert Reich\, decided to
  invite 30 and 80-year-olds to gather in conversation about the country. T
 he Work in Progress invites strangers from across generations and geograph
 ies to engage in candid discussions about America’s ever-evolving democr
 acy\, identity\, and definitions of community\, and where the heck we go f
 rom here. Their idea is to visit six states in six months\, convening a wi
 de variety of Americans to play a simple game: Ask each other questions\, 
 and take the time to listen. What emerges in The Work in Progress is an un
 usual and provocative glimpse of America in its 250th year\, and an invita
 tion to all Americans to pull up a chair\, invite a stranger\, and lend an
  ear.\n\nSources: Vermont.com\, Vermont Public
LOCATION:Main Street Landing Film House\, 60 Lake St
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/the-work-i
 n-progress-25-08-2026-15-10-26
END:VEVENT
